\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Isometric exchanges and a prescribed group action}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{V,V'} be finite-dimensional complex Hermitian spaces
with self-adjoint \vocabk{gradings}{fdip-013N}. Give them
unitary actions of groups #{G,G'} preserving the sign spaces
and fix a homomorphism #{\alpha:G\to G'}. Choose
#{\sigma} to be the identity or complex conjugation.
An isometric #{\sigma}-semilinear
\vocabk{equivariant sector exchange}{fdip-018A} from #{V}
onto #{V'} exists precisely when there are bijective
#{\sigma}-semilinear maps #{b_+:V_+\to V'_-} and
#{b_-:V_-\to V'_+} satisfying, for every #{g\in G},
##{\begin{aligned}
 b_+\rho_+(g)&=\rho'_-(\alpha(g))b_+,\\
 b_-\rho_-(g)&=\rho'_+(\alpha(g))b_-.
\end{aligned}}
For conjugation these are equivalences from the conjugate
source modules to the corresponding pulled-back target modules.
For a single graded action and #{\alpha=\mathrm{id}}, an
intertwining bijection #{V_+\to V_-} of the chosen scalar type
suffices, using its inverse on the other summand.}

\proof{
\p{An isometric exchange restricts to the indicated bijections.
Conversely, in the conjugate-linear case let #{c:v\mapsto\bar v}
be the canonical antiunitary from a summand to its conjugate
space, with action #{c(v)\mapsto c(\rho(g)v)}.
Write #{b=Lc} on that summand. Then #{L} is linear,
bijective and intertwines this action with the target action
pulled back by #{\alpha}. In the linear case use #{L=b}.}

\p{For either source action #{r} and target action #{s}, the
identity #{Lr(g)=s(g)L} and unitarity give
#{r(g)^\dagger L^\dagger Lr(g)=L^\dagger L}. Thus the source
action preserves the eigenspaces of the positive definite
#{L^\dagger L}. On its eigenspace with eigenvalue
#{\lambda>0}, multiply by #{\lambda^{-1/2}} to form
#{(L^\dagger L)^{-1/2}}. This commutes with the action.
The resulting #{L(L^\dagger L)^{-1/2}} is onto and preserves
the inner product, as in \ref{fdip-017J}. Composing with #{c}
when required gives the desired isometry on each summand.
The self-adjoint gradings make both decompositions orthogonal,
so the two isometries assemble to an isometry of the sums.}

\p{For the same untwisted action the inverse of an isometric
intertwiner of the chosen scalar type is an isometric
intertwiner of that type in the reverse direction. With a
prescribed #{\alpha} the two displayed equations apply to
their respective restrictions.}
}
